[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `err09_tag_struct_size_mismatch` sweeps `collided` over every byte offset of a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `[u8; 40]` buffer, so `A`/`B` are misaligned for most offsets. The original Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `collided` used plain dereferences:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m c2CircletoCircle(unsafe { *(A as *const c2Circle) }, unsafe { *(B as *const c2Circle) })
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A plain `*ptr` in Rust requires the pointer to be aligned. The C has no such
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m requirement — `*(c2Circle *)A` on `const void *A` compiles to ordinary `movss` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `mov` loads that succeed at any address — so a caller may legitimately pass a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m misaligned pointer and the C returns a normal result. The Rust version instead
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m hit `misaligned pointer dereference` and **aborted the process** under debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m assertions (and was UB in release, where LLVM is free to assume alignment).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fixed by switching all four dereferences in `collided` to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `core::ptr::read_unaligned`, which is what actually matches the C's observable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behaviour. Verified: the driver script fails with the old code and passes with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the fix, in both `debug` and `release`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note the failure mode this exposed in the *harness*: because the cdylib is built
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with `panic = "abort"`, the misaligned-pointer check kills the test process
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before libtest can print `test result: FAILED`. `run-tests.sh` therefore judges
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m each run by its **exit status** (via `PIPESTATUS`), never by grepping stdout.
