[38;5;252m[1m## The bug[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mcollided[0m used plain dereferences ([38;5;10m*(A as *const c2Circle)[0m), which in Rust require an aligned pointer. The C does [38;5;10m*(c2Circle *)A[0m on a [38;5;10mconst void *[0m — on x86-64 that lowers to ordinary loads that succeed at [3many[23m address, so a caller may legitimately pass a misaligned pointer and get a normal result. The Rust version instead hit [38;5;10mmisaligned pointer dereference[0m and [1maborted the process[22m under debug assertions (and was UB in release, where LLVM may assume alignment). Fixed by switching all four dereferences to [38;5;10mcore::ptr::read_unaligned[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I also found a flaw in my own harness worth calling out: [38;5;10mcargo test[0m does [1mnot[22m rebuild a [38;5;10mcdylib[0m-only lib target, so my first several "all green" runs were [38;5;10mdlopen[0m-ing a stale [38;5;10m.so[0m. The harness now refuses to run if the [38;5;10m.so[0m is older than any crate input, and [38;5;10mrun-tests.sh[0m builds first. Relatedly, because the cdylib is built [38;5;10mpanic = "abort"[0m, a UB check kills the test process before libtest prints [38;5;10mtest result: FAILED[0m, so the driver judges runs by exit status via [38;5;10mPIPESTATUS[0m, never by grepping stdout.[0m[0m
