[38;5;252m[1m## Why the passes are meaningful[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Since the translation was already nearly correct, passing tests alone prove little, so I ran a mutation battery: 25 mutations injected into [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m, rebuilt and re-tested one at a time. Every behaviour-changing mutation was caught. The 5 survivors are semantically equivalent and unkillable — e.g. [38;5;10m(d0|d1|d2|d3)==0[0m → [38;5;10m(d0+d1+d2+d3)==0[0m is identical when each term is 0/1, and dropping the explicit [38;5;10m0*inf[0m → [38;5;10m0xFFC00000[0m rules is a no-op because Rust's own [38;5;10mf32[0m arithmetic lowers to the same SSE instructions (verified empirically). Details are in [38;5;10mCONFIGS.md[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fragile part of this translation is its hand-written SSE emulation ([38;5;10mmulss[0m/[38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10msubss[0m NaN quieting and dst/src priority), which exists because the C is compiled unoptimized and every FP op is a discrete instruction whose operand order is observable in NaN payloads. I confirmed the emulated order against the C disassembly and then hammered it: exhaustive 4-slot cross-products over a 24-entry NaN/inf/zero/boundary pool, a 1M-iteration uniform-random-bits sweep, a 255×255 exponent grid, and ±4-ULP sweeps around the [38;5;10md2 < r2[0m boundary. Flipping the NaN operand priority in any of the three helpers is caught by 5 tests each.[0m[0m
